Michael P. Masters #2428

a scholar and author known for his work in biblical studies and his critical analysis of ancient astronaut theories, including those of Zecharia Sitchin.


I learned about a close encounter that my dad had. He was a veterinarian in Northeast Ohio, where I grew up, and he was out one night on a call with another guy. So there’s two people, and that scale has more reliability because there’s multiple witnesses. It’s also strange on his scale because they crested the hill; there’s a bright light. This is Amish country, there’s no lights in Amish country, and all of a sudden this bright light darts toward him, hovers just above their truck, darts back to where it was, and straight up into space at incredible speed. This happened before I was born, but I overheard him telling the story to some friends one night. He got Whitley Strieber’s book, Communion, as most people did in the 1980s, and as they should, because it’s a great book. I walked into the living room, the book is sitting on the shelf facing out, and I remember like it was yesterday: I sort of stopped and there was this light, like a white light, and then I saw this image in my mind of an early hominid/chimpanzee-like creature, a modern human, and then that archetypal gray alien from the cover. With this information: "What if they’re related? What if they are us from the future?"

And obviously, that’s why I became a biological anthropologist. People are always like, "So you saw this?" Yeah, it was like a flash, a light. Looking back on it, it’s weird because I know a lot more about these experiences; I’ve talked to people that have had these types of experiences. It was our living room. You come in the front door, and just to the left, there’s this built-in bookshelf. The book was right there. I just turned the corner, saw it, and it just came. I didn’t know shit about evolutionary biology, I didn’t know... it was just in my mind, just this white light, just an image. And there’s that image of the three faces, and the question: could they be us from the future? A lot of people are like, "How did you get into UFOs? You’re a biological anthropologist." It’s the opposite: I got into biological anthropology to research this question of whether they could be us from the future.

It was some tasking. I think Rupert Sheldrake said it: people don’t have ideas, ideas have people. I think that was like, "Hey, go do this thing," and I did. That’s why we’re here now talking about it. Obviously, you’ve got to be careful about selection bias and confirmation bias. I didn’t go into grad school, I also didn’t tell anybody in grad school I was there because they would have kicked me out, but I went into it with an open mind, but I was there to study these things because of that event when I was eight years old.

It is a problem—the confirmation bias—it is a problem with wanting it to be real, and I struggle with that because I desperately want it to be real. Every time I talk to someone, I’ve talked to a bunch of people that are air-quote "whistleblowers," and some of them I think for sure have been sent in here to distribute this information—no doubt, for sure, because it’s a great place to do it. I’ll listen to you; I’ll entertain almost anything, which is great. We need that. Obviously, there’s people that are going to take advantage of it, but I think it’s also important for me to say, I’m not convinced. I don’t know how much of this is horseshit, but it’s not zero.

I do draw from J. Allen Hynek—in his book The UFO Experience, he lists out how we all should approach this based on the reliability scale and the strangeness scale. Jacques Vallée also drew from that, which helped him develop it as part of the Invisible College and all of his work. Regardless of my own personal discernment, my second book, The Extraterrestrial Model, is about 30 case studies—15 main case studies, but then I pull in others—and it explores the different theories; obviously, the main one being this extra-tempestal idea, this "future" idea. By the way, the word of the day today was "anachronistic," and I was like, "Man, that would have been a way better word than extraterrestrial, which everybody struggles with. I could have called them 'anacronauts.'" Doesn’t that sound cool?

One of the most commonly reported things across all cases, regardless of whether you think it’s bullshit or you think this definitely happened, is they really want our sperm; they really need or want our reproductive material, our gametes. When I wrote my first book, I started in 2012, published in 2019. Right at the end, I did an interview and somebody was like, "Have you heard of Jim Penniston?" I’m like, "No." Which is kind of a failing on my behalf, I’ll admit that. It turns out it was the Rendlesham Forest incident. He touched this craft, he got this binary code, and when deciphering the binary code, they legitimately, specifically said, "We are you from the future. We’re having problems with reproduction." He underwent hypnotic regression: "We’re having problems with reproduction, and we need this genetic material to help ourselves."

A lot of people are like, "Well, why are they coming back and doing stuff to us?" I think they’re coming back and getting stuff from us because of problems they’re having, largely related to what you were talking about earlier with reduced sperm counts, the problems with female infertility. What if we do try to create the perfect human specimen, or we try to cure these genetic diseases through genetic manipulation (CRISPR), and we screw something up? We might have to come back; we can’t go to another planet. There aren’t people on these planets. We can’t go and sample gametes from these other places. We might have to go into our past to get those wild-type, unmanipulated gametes in order to fix these problems.

That’s a crazy level of technological sophistication—the ability to venture back in time and somehow or another not fuck up the timeline that’s leading to... I mean, this is the problem that’s always been theorized about time travel. Anything that you do, if you went back in time, any interactions, you would completely change how the future would play out in the Many-Worlds interpretation. That idea is unfortunately very pervasive, mostly because of Back to the Future, which I think ruins the brains of most people—mine too, certainly in my generation. But most physicists don’t agree on many things, but most agree that we live in what’s called a block universe landscape, block time, where if you imagine all moments from the very beginning of the Big Bang to the end of the universe—where all matter disappears into a black hole or contracts or whatever it does—all moments are already there. They exist as this massive four-dimensional block of all moments, all world lines, everything.

So, you go back into the past as you perceive it, you can do whatever you want; you can walk around, step on butterflies, you can slap people on the face, kick over dinosaurs or whatever (I don’t think we can go back that far), but you could do anything you want, and it doesn’t change anything because you’re going back in the block universe and doing those things you were always already going to do. And when you get home, everything’s the same, because that was already their past. To everybody that stayed behind, that was already their past; it was only the future for you to go back and do those things that you were already going to do. And then you just went and did them; get home, everything’s the same, because you were always going to do those things in the first place.

That’s bizarre. That’s hard to swallow. If that is the actual model of the universe—and again, I can only work in writing these books from what we know now—clearly there’s a lot of things we don’t know. I’m not claiming to know anything beyond what we can know right now. But physicists, despite not knowing what time is, know it’s an emergent phenomenon—there’s something more fundamental that time comes from—but they do agree on this block universe model, and in that case, there is no paradox.

I shouldn’t say they all agree, because there is the Many-Worlds interpretation of quantum mechanics where, if you went back in that situation, it would be changing—you would be changing the timeline. Would it be changing your timeline or would it be changing a different timeline? That is the question, and how would you know? There’s more paradoxes with changing things than not changing things.

Why do you confidently state that you don’t think that they can go back to the dinosaur age? Pumas are an established animal to have, like some cryptozoological creature like Bigfoot, that’s undetected. It’s fun. I want to believe, but I don’t really. Well, that’s like in the ocean, like they find some big fish or some big whale, and you’re like, "I don’t know, dude, we never knew it was down there." They think aliens could live in the ocean, too—that’s how much that was. Jeremy Corbel was talking about that the other day. It’s hard to say what kind of information he’s getting, how accurate it is, but they’re basically saying—think about the movie The Abyss, where the aliens lived underwater because that’s probably what’s going on—that there’s some sort of alien base underwater or some meeting place, or that’s where they hide, that’s where they go to be undetected.

Do you think you’re going to see it in your lifetime? Bigfoot or aliens? Aliens. There’s video that is impossible to ignore; there’s some things that are impossible to ignore. And then there’s eyewitness testimonies of people who are rock-solid, totally dependable, like fighter pilots, who have seen some things. The best example is this guy, Commander David Fravor, who was off the coast of San Diego in 2004, and this is corroborated by multiple points of data. They had this tracking system that tracks things above Earth’s atmosphere, and they found this thing went from above 60,000 feet above sea level to 50 feet above sea level in less than a second. They have no idea how it’s doing this; there’s no method of propulsion that’s visible. They followed this thing, they locked onto it, it blocked their radar, their tracking systems rather, and then it jetted off at an impossible rate of speed. And then the Nimitz relocated this thing at their CAP point. The CAP point is where the fighter pilots, when they’re doing this exercise, are supposed to meet at this very specific point, and that’s where this thing had gone. Like this thing had read their manifest, knew their plans, and knew where they were going. That is way more compelling to me than Bigfoot.

Some days I go back and forth. Sometimes I think maybe it’s some sort of government drone that’s so sophisticated, it’s totally top secret, and if we ever go to war with China, that’s when they’re going to break it out. And then sometimes I think what they are is something that’s monitoring us and making sure we don’t blow ourselves up, making sure that we make this journey from territorial apes with thermonuclear weapons into some sort of an advanced intergalactic society. And that there’s a very crucial moment where the instincts of these tribal people—which is all of us, all human beings are these tribal, territorial people—where you have to keep them from sabotaging any possible future progress by blowing themselves up, by killing each other.

What if we talk to them, and then when you say that, they go, "Yeah, that was it." Is that easy? Not that easy, but like, you just go, "Is that it?" You go, "Yeah, that’s about it. We’re trying to keep you guys from blowing yourselves up." Maybe that’s the natural thing that you see when civilizations advance—there’s a very precarious moment where they have the capability of blowing themselves up, but they also have these instincts to control resources and take over territories. They still have those instincts, but they have to bypass that; they have to figure out how. And so that’s when aliens start circling and just keeping an eye on us, because this is that problem. That’s what you would hope, right, instead of them attacking us? Yes, because then I think if they were going to attack us, they would have already done it. If they really could do all those things, they could probably take out our power grid pretty easy. Apparently, the stories are that they’re able to shut down nuclear launches and nuclear facilities, and they hover over military bases, and they’ll just shut everything down just to let you know, "Hey, bitch, I could just flip that switch, so don’t get too fucking squirrely out there."

If you think about what an alien is, in terms of our iconic image of an alien—like the Spielberg Close Encounters of the Third Kind alien—they seem like what we’d assume a human being would eventually become, right? And if these things are tiny human beings, we’re smaller than they’ve ever been before, we’re weaker than we’ve ever been before, and there seems to be a trend in that direction. And this trend seems to be amplified by our technological progression, our lack of need for muscle strength, and our lack of need for violence. We’re moving in a society to try to get away from all the things that we think are abhorrent about human beings and the terrible behaviors that we have. If we one day do give birth to some sort of an artificial being—like Marshall McLuhan’s quote, "We are the sex organs of the machine world"—that one day we...

Okay, I’ll buy this. McLuhan was brilliant, and that quote has always been one of my favorites. Because what are we doing when we’re constantly technologically innovating? We’re constantly looking for faster cars, better computers, bigger screens, faster—more resolution, more pixels, more this, more that, higher bandwidth, 5G, 10G. What are we doing? We’re moving 100% towards technological innovation. If you looked at this species from afar, and if you weren’t a part of it, you would say, "What does this species do? Oh, they make things. They make things better every year." Beehives are the same fucking thing you see 10 years ago. You go by, you see a beehive—it’s amazing, it’s cool—but they’re the same fucking thing. They figured out how to do it; they make a beehive. We don’t do that. We make better things constantly. And at some point, I think that technology is going to fuse with us, and we’re going to become... it’s already happening. Elon Musk talked about it on my podcast, that we are cyborgs. You just carry it in your pocket—it’s a phone. It answers any question you want, you can talk to it, it’ll give you the answers instantaneously, it navigates you, it has all your phone numbers, all your contacts, you can get a hold of people. People are listening to you through it. It’s connecting us in ways—even involuntarily, haptics, that kind of thing. It’s also getting on your wrist—how many people have Apple Watches? They’re put on their wrist. And that’s only because we can’t integrate them yet, but you know that point is coming 100%.

What would you do if you were a hyper-intelligence? Would you do the work yourself, or would you create some cool things called humans to do it for you? Would you create things that are cybernetic organisms to come in with machines and do it for you? If you were a hyper-intelligence that has kind of changed like you’ve described, you’d probably create workers, right? I don’t think it’s necessarily that, but that is the way a conspiracy theorist would look at it. The way I would look at it is there’s obviously a progression going on, a biological progression, some sort of integration with technology, some sort of imperative. This need for technological innovation is inescapable; everyone has it. And I think it’s attached to materialism in some sort of a strange way, because so many people work so hard to get new things, and god, that seems so illogical and preposterous, and it makes people unhappy, and depression’s on the rise, but nobody seems to be able to stop it. Why is that?

Well, maybe it’s because we are the electronic caterpillars that give birth to the butterfly. Maybe that’s what we’re doing. Maybe what our job is to do is to make some sort of a cocoon. We don’t even know we’re doing it while we’re doing it. Do you think a caterpillar is aware? "Hey, caterpillar, what are you doing, man?" "Just doing my thing, it’s my job. I have to make a cocoon, then I’m going to become like a butterfly." This could be a natural part of evolution; it could be that we’re just supposed to do this and make the jump to some sort of mechanized, non-biological...

An orangutan is fishing with a spear. They’ve figured out how to fish with spears. They’ve imitated human beings doing it, and now they do it. They’re not trained orangutans; they’re wild orangutans. There’s primatologists—biologists—that believe that monkeys and chimps and some of the great apes are moving into the Stone Age, that they’ve currently entered the Stone Age, that they’re not staying what they were 100,000 years ago or 500,000 years ago, but they’re actively using tools and they’re experimenting with different ways to use those tools, and they’re making tools out of stone, making tools out of sticks. Well, this might just be what happens.

I was driving to LA this morning; I had a doctor’s appointment, so I was on the 405 at 8:00 in the morning. Like, Jesus Christ, this is so crazy. When you’re on the 405 in LA at 8:00 in the morning, you see literally a million cars. But I’m in a Tesla, so I have it on autopilot. I’m sitting there, listening to a podcast, I barely have my hand on the wheel, I’m not touching shit. This car is driving me along. It’s so much less stress, by the way, to do it that way. So it encourages you to innovate, it encourages you to embrace this new technology. I’ve got this giant screen, it’s showing me the navigation in front of me—"Oh, I’ll be there five minutes early, excellent"—and I’m listening to a podcast wirelessly, Bluetooth streaming from my phone. I pulled that podcast, which came out today, out of the fucking sky, and I’m listening to it. This is irresistible stuff.

Ted Kaczynski was right. This is something that I think about sometimes when I get really high. Ted Kaczynski was a part of the Harvard LSD studies; this has been proven. They cooked his fucking brain when he was at Harvard, and then when he went over to Berkeley and became a professor, his goal was to make enough money so that he could implement this program and live in the woods and then write his manifesto and start killing people that were involved in propagating technology.

I want to be very careful with that word "conspiracy theorist." What I was saying to you was, we terraform our Earth, right? We change the environment, we do all this innovation. What is stopping us from thinking that that’s not being done on a much bigger level—on a cosmic level? I’m telling you that there is something here. There’s a fact: there’s a craft, they’re here, they’re not ours. So the question is: what is that about? I think it’s much more likely that the same way we observe chimps and we observe that they are now in the Stone Age, that they’re observing us, and that they’re recognizing that there is a pattern, that there are steps that happen.

Even when you’re driving a car and you’re stomping on the gas, you didn’t invent the fucking engine, you didn’t invent tires. There’s all these things that were involved in the creation of this thing that is really outside of your grasp of understanding, but yet you have the ability to use it. Like a person with a gun—"I’m just going to bang-bang-bang people"—you didn’t invent a gun. You have almost no responsibility. You could just flippantly use these things, which is why we were very childlike in our actions, because we haven’t had to earn the responsibility.

Why do we need oil? Why can’t we just burn wood and stay home? Why can’t we grow chickens and food in the backyard? We fucking can; people do do it, but we decide to make that almost impossible. Our preferred way of living is to stuff everyone into a very small area where no one grows anything other than weed. This is what LA is—LA is 20 million people with as many hard surfaces as you can. But we’re a blink, man. Something hits, but we don’t think that way. You think in terms of your own life, you think in terms of what you want and what you need right now. We are in many ways this combination of this weird, primitive, ape-like thing with the ability to calculate and manipulate our world and our environment, that makes us wholly unique, on top of that with existential angst and fear.

I don’t think they are us, but I think they are what happens when things keep going. 70 years ago, 15 years ago, we wouldn’t see it; now these exact things at 20,000 mph are documented. Somebody has a non-reactionary, fuel-propulsion system. Now, are they aliens or some subsect of humans? Throughout history, there are these indications of ships, of craft.

The Miracle of Fatima—that was considered a real miracle by the church, and the three kids were getting messages. Bigger and bigger groups gathered, where there was over 100,000 people in 1917. Dozens of thousands, if not hundreds, see a mass sighting of something. We’re talking people that were religious, people that weren’t religious, doctors at the time. Something happened that was so intense—some people thought it was religious, that it was a visitation—but other people saw it as technological. This is not an airplane, this is not a weather balloon, this is not a blip. It has performance well beyond the airplanes that we were flying.

If the radars are right, and Commander Fravor saw this thing moving like a ping-pong ball in a glass of water, it’s gravity-propelled. That’s why you can make those maneuvers without exploding. Okay, then distance becomes completely irrelevant for travel. You’re literally warping time and space.

There are studies being done right now on materials associated with UFOs, actual metals that have come off of, you could say, crashes. Jacques Vallée has 17 samples. He first created a computer program for the United States military for the study of UFOs for Project Blue Book. He’s an old-school guy; they modeled him after the character in the Close Encounters movie. He’s had more access to government databases than any of us. He went around and collected samples from known crashes or things that came off.

I actually took some to New Mexico and had five scientists study it about five years ago. What we were having the scientists look at is isotopically and elementally, what are the elements that are in this? Are they engineered? From what I understand, there’s atomic layering. There are levels of—like we do with graphene—we lay down these layers that are basically oriented at a certain degree; that’s how you get a superconductor. If you take a piece of graphene and it’s highly machined so it doesn’t deviate from that atomic layering, you can push it right through ice like a hot knife through butter. It’s the weirdest feeling in the world. So that is a meta-material. It doesn’t occur naturally; humans created it. And it has special properties because of the way it’s atomically layered. So if these materials are from somewhere else, we suspect that they’re going to be highly machined, that they’re going to be created for special use.

I’ve watched Travis Walton. Very compelling in his descriptions of his encounter with these beings. One of the things he was saying was they were talking to him, but it wasn’t with words. The information was flowing faster because it was, like, boom, right away. Yes, they were explaining things to him in some sort of a telepathic way. And it was mind-blowing to him. I think that’s probably the future of communication.

I believe Bob Lazar. I believe what he’s saying is true. He had one moment where he was passing by a window, and he said he saw something that was small, and these people were standing over it. He doesn’t know if it was an alien, or whether it was some sort of a form that they were trying—like a doll or something—to represent the size of an alien, or some sort of model. But he says it was very compartmentalized. One of the problems they were trying to figure out was how to back-engineer these devices, these ships, but the problem was they weren’t allowed to communicate with other people outside of this very limited group. The people that were involved in the metallurgy were not allowed to communicate with the people that were involved in the propulsion system. You can’t improve like that; you need to share ideas.

One of the things that he talked about came to fruition: this thing called element 115. That was not really proven until the 2000s; they used a particle collider and created this thing. He was saying that wherever these beings are from, they have figured out a way to utilize a stable version of this element 115, and that’s how they propel themselves.

Zecharia Sitchin’s work—that’s all the Sumerian text. It’s some fascinating stuff because it’s all from 6,000 years ago. He is very controversial; there’s a website called Sitchiniswrong.com. But even if his translations are off, there’s still a lot of really confusing stuff about ancient Sumer. One of the things is their depictions of the solar system; they had a depiction 6,000 years ago that shows the sun in the center and it shows all of the known planets, and they’re relatively accurate in terms of the size.

There’s clearly knowledge that we’ve lost in the past. Just think about the Library of Alexandria. Imagine the knowledge that was there before it burned. Medicine, botany, science—it’s just crazy to think of. Greek fire is a weapon that was used in naval battles; historians still don’t know exactly how it works, but it was so destructive. They cannot recreate it. It’s clearly a technology that we have lost. The pyramids are a perfect example of technology loss. They were originally covered in smooth, polished limestone. That would probably be insane to look at. 2,300,000 stones, some of them cut from quarries hundreds of miles away, all perfectly aligned. Most scholars would say they use a ramp, but if you think about it, the construction of that ramp would need to be at an angle that could be so difficult to do it would challenge the construction of the pyramid itself.

Bob Lazar said that one of the things they were teaching him at Area 51 was that human beings are the product of accelerated evolution, and that these creatures occasionally come back to check upon our progress. Maybe these beings that are very far advanced come back and they see these creatures that are pretty close, and they give them a little boost.

I’m probably like 90% convinced [that aliens are real]. I leave room for bullshit. If they’re here, they’re not going to let us... that’s what I think. They’re going to swoop in and go, "Hey, hey, hey."

I was in upstate New York when I was probably 16. I was looking out the window with my cousin, and it’s just a light that just stays there, and then just fucking goes off. You can’t explain it. And then in Tarzana, I saw what sounded corny, like a classic force field. It was kind of like a dome in a circular shape, and it just went like this and went back in. I was there with four other people, and they saw it too. If aliens are visiting us, they probably just know how to evade detection.

I think there’s enough talk about it now that we’re probably going to find out what it is in our lifetime. I think the government withheld information for whatever reason—maybe they think people are scared, maybe they think it’s a threat to national security. What did the Pentagon just say literally two fucking days ago? They’ve recovered crafts that are not from this world. Not made on Earth. They’re basically saying, "We don’t know what the fuck it is, but we didn’t make it." That’s bananas.

Maybe they figured out a way to make life so that you can transfer your consciousness into some eternal mechanical...
